# Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by each machine's git.
#
# Without this file the answer depends on whoever committed: a checkout made
# on Windows holds CRLF, and a commit made from a shell with core.autocrlf
# unset stores those CRLF bytes -- so the next diff reports every line of
# every file as changed, and the real change is buried in it. That happened
# once, which is why this file exists.
#
# text=auto: the repository always stores LF, whatever the working tree holds.
# The working tree keeps the platform's own convention, so Windows checkouts
# are CRLF as before and nothing on anybody's disk has to change.
* text=auto

# The two files whose line endings are not a convention but a requirement.
# build.sh is read by /bin/sh, which fails on the first compound statement if
# the lines end in CR -- reporting a syntax error in code that is correct.
# A .bat is read by cmd.exe, which wants CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never touched: a converted byte in any of these corrupts the file.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.aoe binary
*.aoec binary
*.hdf5 binary
*.h5 binary
